ash for Windows, via Chocolatey
===============================

Why this package needs a Python index at install time
-----------------------------------------------------

The package bundles ASH's own wheel and nothing else. Its chocolateyinstall.ps1
creates a virtualenv under %ProgramData%\ash\venv and installs that wheel into it,
which resolves ASH's 27 runtime dependencies from whatever Python index the machine
is configured for.

This is not an oversight. ASH's own code may ship in a package published by the
project; third-party code may not. Carrying exactly one wheel is the guard that
keeps this package on the right side of that line, because it can be checked by
counting the files under tools\wheels\ rather than by judging each dependency one
at a time. That count is asserted in three places: build.ps1 before packing,
chocolateyinstall.ps1 on the machine being installed to, and
verify-on-windows.ps1 against the built .nupkg.

Chocolatey's own convention pulls the other way. A great many packages vendor
their binaries into tools\ and install from there with no network at all, and that
is exactly the shape this package must not take. Nothing but ASH's wheel goes
under tools\wheels\.

Building a self-contained offline package for your own machines is fine, and the
commands below are the supported way to do it. The line is about what the project
publishes, not about what the bytes are allowed to do.

On a machine with no reachable index, stage one before installing:

  pip download automated-security-helper==<version> -d C:\ash-wheels
  # then, before choco install, as Administrator:
  mkdir %ProgramData%\pip
  > %ProgramData%\pip\pip.ini echo [global]
  >>%ProgramData%\pip\pip.ini echo no-index = true
  >>%ProgramData%\pip\pip.ini echo find-links = C:\ash-wheels

Three names on PATH, and why the long one exists
------------------------------------------------

The package installs a shim for every console script the wheel declares, which is
currently three:

  ash                        canonical, and the one to use.
  ashv3                      deprecated. It works and warns once on stderr. The
                             name pins a major version, so it reads wrong the
                             moment v4 exists.
  automated-security-helper  kept indefinitely and silent.

The long name is not redundancy for its own sake, and Windows is the reason it
exists. MSYS2 ships the Almquist shell as ash.exe. On a machine that has MSYS2,
Git for Windows with the MSYS tools on PATH, or anything else that provides that
name earlier in PATH than the Chocolatey bin directory, `ash scan` reaches the
shell instead of this program and fails with `Illegal option --`. That has already
happened in this project's CI. When it happens, use automated-security-helper;
nothing else claims that name.

Two flags worth knowing because they are easy to get backwards: -V is --version,
and -v is --verbose. The bash entry point that ASH used to ship had -v for
--version, which is why this is called out.

Where things are installed
--------------------------

  <ChocolateyInstall>\lib\ash\tools\wheels\   ASH's wheel, the package's only
                                              payload. Chocolatey manages it.
  %ProgramData%\ash\venv\                     created by chocolateyinstall.ps1
  %ProgramData%\ash\installed-shims.txt       the shim names, for the uninstaller
  <ChocolateyInstall>\bin\ash.exe             a shim, plus one per console script

The venv is built on the machine rather than shipped, because a virtualenv records
absolute paths and the interpreter ABI of the machine that created it. It is
deliberately NOT placed inside the Chocolatey package directory: after
chocolateyinstall.ps1 returns, Chocolatey scans that directory for executables and
shims each one it finds, so a venv there would put python.exe, pythonw.exe,
pip.exe, and pip3.exe on every user's PATH ahead of whatever Python they chose.
Suppressing that requires a .ignore file per executable, which is a filename list
that has to track whatever the venv module and pip create.

The uninstaller removes the venv, because pip created it after install and
Chocolatey does not track files an install script wrote.

The python3 dependency, and its upper bound
-------------------------------------------

The nuspec declares python3 in the range [3.10,3.14). The floor is ASH's own
requires-python. The ceiling is there because ash-unified-ci.yml exercises 3.10
through 3.13 and nothing above: without it, Chocolatey would satisfy the dependency
with whatever python3 is newest on the day of install, and the first symptom would
be pip failing to resolve a dependency that has no wheel for that interpreter,
reported to the user as a broken ASH install rather than as an untested
interpreter.

chocolateyinstall.ps1 does not assume the dependency put the right interpreter
first on PATH. A machine can carry an older Python from a previous install, from
the Microsoft Store stub, or from a conda environment, and whichever is earliest
on PATH wins. So it probes the py launcher for 3.13, 3.12, 3.11, and 3.10 in turn
before falling back to `python` and `python3`, and takes the first that reports a
version in range. The py launcher entries come first because they resolve through
the registry rather than through PATH.

Scanners are not installed by this package
------------------------------------------

ASH drives third-party scanners; it does not contain them. After installing:

  ash dependencies install              # everything ASH can provision
  ash dependencies install --tool grype # or one at a time

This package is not published anywhere
--------------------------------------

Nothing in packaging/ submits to community.chocolatey.org. This directory exists so
the package can be built and exercised; publishing it is a separate decision nobody
has made. A submission would also need the Chocolatey-specific metadata the
community repository requires (packageSourceUrl, docsUrl, bugTrackerUrl), which
ash.nuspec deliberately omits because the NuGet XSD does not declare those
elements and validate-nuspec.sh is the only check that runs without a Windows
machine.

How this is tested
------------------

  bash packaging/chocolatey/validate-nuspec.sh
      Validates ash.nuspec against NuGet's published XSD. Runs anywhere xmllint
      does. Rejects two deliberately broken copies of the file first, so a run that
      prints "validates" cannot be a validator that checks nothing.

  pwsh packaging/chocolatey/verify-on-windows.ps1
      Builds the package, installs it, asserts all three names resolve to
      Chocolatey shims, scans a fixture containing a planted secret, parses the
      SARIF and fails unless the result count is nonzero, then uninstalls and fails
      if the venv survived. Needs Windows and an already-built wheel in dist/.
      .github/workflows/ash-package.yml runs it on windows-latest, whose image
      ships Chocolatey preinstalled.
